  <h2>Introduction</h2>
  <p>
    This Charter sets out the rights of every patient receiving care at St.
    Paul's Mission Hospital. It is aligned with the <strong
      >Kenya Health Act 2017</strong
    >, the
    <strong> Kenya Patients' Rights Charter (2013)</strong>, and the ethical
    teachings of the Catholic Church on the dignity of the human person.
  </p>
  <p>
    These rights apply to every patient without discrimination on the basis of
    age, gender, tribe, religion, disability, sexual orientation, social status,
    or ability to pay. They apply equally to outpatient, inpatient, maternity,
    emergency, and community health services.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>

  <h3>1. Right to respectful and dignified care</h3>
  <p>
    You have the right to be treated with courtesy, respect, and dignity by
    every member of staff at every point of care. This includes being addressed
    by your preferred name, having your privacy protected, and receiving care in
    a clean and safe environment.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Right to information</h3>
  <p>
    You have the right to clear, accurate, and timely information about your
    diagnosis, the purpose of tests and procedures, available treatment options,
    expected outcomes, and the cost of care — in a language and format you can
    understand.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Right to informed consent</h3>
  <p>
    No procedure, treatment, or intervention will be carried out without your
    informed consent, except in a life-threatening emergency where you cannot
    give consent. You have the right to refuse or withdraw consent at any time.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Right to privacy and confidentiality</h3>
  <p>
    Your medical information, including your identity and the fact that you are
    receiving care at SPMH, is confidential. It will only be disclosed with your
    written consent, or when required by law (for example, in cases of
    notifiable disease or court order).
  </p>

  <h3>5. Right to a second opinion and referral</h3>
  <p>
    You have the right to request a second opinion from another clinician at
    SPMH, or to request referral to another facility. If we cannot provide the
    care you need, we will refer you promptly with complete medical records.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Right to access your medical records</h3>
  <p>
    You have the right to access your medical records, to obtain a copy, and to
    request correction of factual inaccuracies, subject to administrative
    procedures and applicable law.
  </p>

  <h3>7. Right to complain</h3>
  <p>
    You have the right to lodge a complaint, or to have a family member or
    advocate do so on your behalf, without fear of retaliation or reduced care.
    Complaints are handled according to the procedure in our <a
      href='/governance/service-charter/'>Service Charter</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h3>8. Right to family and community support</h3>
  <p>
    You have the right to have a family member or trusted person present during
    care, where clinically appropriate, and to be visited according to hospital
    visiting hours.
  </p>

  <h3>9. Right to spiritual and pastoral care</h3>
  <p>
    You have the right to practice your faith, receive spiritual support, and
    access pastoral care. Chaplaincy services are available through the Catholic
    Diocese of Homa Bay, and spiritual care is offered respectfully to patients
    of all faiths and none.
  </p>

  <h3>10. Right to safe and competent care</h3>
  <p>
    You have the right to care delivered by qualified, licensed professionals
    following national clinical guidelines, and to be treated in a facility that
    meets health and safety standards.
  </p>

  <h3>11. Right to fair billing and information on costs</h3>
  <p>
    You have the right to know the expected cost of your treatment in advance,
    to receive an itemized bill, and to have your insurance or SHA entitlements
    applied correctly. Financial hardship should be raised with the billing
    office — no patient is denied emergency care due to inability to pay.
  </p>

  <h3>12. Right to a child-friendly and disability-inclusive environment</h3>
  <p>
    Children, elderly patients, and persons with disabilities have the right to
    an environment adapted to their needs, including accessible facilities,
    adapted communication, and the presence of a guardian or caregiver.
  </p>

  <h2>Your responsibilities</h2>
  <p>Alongside these rights, we ask that you:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Provide accurate and complete information about your health, medications,
      and medical history.
    </li>
    <li>Follow the agreed treatment plan, or tell us if you cannot.</li>
    <li>Treat hospital staff, other patients, and visitors with respect.</li>
    <li>
      Observe hospital rules on visiting hours, noise, and infection control.
    </li>
    <li>
      Meet your financial obligations to the extent you are able, and discuss
      difficulties early.
    </li>
    <li>
      Inform staff of any change in your condition, or if you wish to change
      your treatment decision.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to make a complaint</h2>
  <p>
    Complaints may be made verbally or in writing to any member of staff, to the
    nurse-in-charge, or to the Hospital Administrator. All complaints are logged
    and investigated. See the <a href='/governance/service-charter/'
      >Service Charter</a
    > for the full escalation process.
  </p>

